					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-2157326#post-2764624</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-2157326/scp-5417-j#post-2764624</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Fri, 10 Mar 2017 17:26:05 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Scorpion451</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>1918720</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>I've been percolating on this as well. Current thought bubbles have included &quot;the anti<a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-662">Mr. Deeds</a>&quot;, taking the concept into surreal horror (because it suddenly gets a lot less funny if &quot;crawls around like a spider&quot; means <em>Exorcist</em>-style), and tying it into the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reynard">Reynard fables</a> somehow, but I haven't quite figured out how to get anywhere really interesting with it.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-2157326#post-2764597</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-2157326/scp-5417-j#post-2764597</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Fri, 10 Mar 2017 16:52:30 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>WrongJohnSilver</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>2000367</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>I did once draft a -J that described French as a memetic effect that convinced people that they were all speaking the same language, even though they clearly weren't.</p> <p>The final punchline was that a similar effect was happening for English, too (A Georgian, a Geordie, and a Glaswegian all claimed to speak &quot;the language of Shakespeare,&quot; despite the fact the three couldn't understand each other, nor could any of them understand Shakespeare).</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
